# Nothing here on purpose. Read this before adding `CHANGELOG.md merge=union`.
#
# ## The problem it was meant to solve
#
# Every PR adds a dated entry to the top of CHANGELOG.md, so any two open PRs
# edit the same region and the second to merge conflicts. That is not merely
# tedious: a CONFLICTING PR runs no `pull_request` workflows at all, and the
# checks still shown are stale ones from an older head, green and describing
# code that is gone. So a guaranteed conflict on a log file was switching off
# every gate in the repository on every second PR.
#
# `merge=union` was added for that on 2026-09-21 and removed the same day.
# What it actually bought, measured on the three PRs that followed it:
#
#   - It did NOT stop GitHub marking a PR CONFLICTING. #342 and #347 both went
#     conflicting with this file on `main`. Mergeability is computed on
#     GitHub's servers and does not read `.gitattributes`, so the dangerous
#     half of the problem was untouched.
#   - Locally it worked only from the SECOND merge onward, because git reads
#     attributes from the branch being merged INTO and the first merge is the
#     one delivering the file.
#   - And it broke `format`, which is a required check, in a way no author can
#     fix on their branch.
#
# ## That last one is why it is gone
#
# Union concatenates both sides' lines for a conflicting hunk, so where two
# entries meet it produces no blank line between the last bullet and the next
# `###` heading, and Prettier requires one. Running `npm run format` and
# committing does not help: GitHub recomputes the merge from the base for
# `refs/pull/<n>/merge`, which is what CI checks out, and union re-applies
# there and drops the blank line again. Observed as a one-byte diff between a
# branch that passes `prettier --check` locally and a merge commit that fails
# it, with no edit available that survives.
#
# So the driver cost a required check its fixability and bought a convenience
# from the second local merge onward. Resolving CHANGELOG.md by hand takes
# seconds; an unfixable red does not.
#
# The real fix for the underlying conflict is not a merge driver: it is not
# putting every PR's entry at the top of one file. A `changelog.d/` directory
# with one file per change, assembled on release, has no shared region to
# conflict over. That is a change to a documented workflow rather than a line
# here, which is why it is written down instead of done.
